**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), London–van der Waals notes, page 4; [Particle–substrate interactions — Don Rimai, edited by Goodarz Ahmadi](../sources/Particle-Substrate-Interactions-Rimai.lingua#r_67MEFYZ26RD2ERAEKSVC0ACCQM), competing interactions.

A Hamaker constant belongs to a material pair and an intervening medium. In one approximate combining model,

A₁₃₂≈(√A₁₁−√A₃₃)(√A₂₂−√A₃₃).

Here the Aᵢᵢ use the same reference-medium convention. The approximation shows why replacing air by a liquid can change the dispersion interaction; it is not a substitute for the frequency-dependent dielectric treatment of a real layered surface. Repulsion is possible in suitable dissimilar systems, so an attractive constant should not be assumed universally.

For hypothetical A₁₁=4×10⁻²⁰ J, A₂₂=9×10⁻²⁰ J and A₃₃=1×10⁻²⁰ J, the estimate is 2×10⁻²⁰ J. Changing only A₃₃ to 6.25×10⁻²⁰ J gives −0.25×10⁻²⁰ J. These are arithmetic examples, not tabulated materials or a cleaning prescription.

**Work:** Check the units of each square root and the final product. Compare the identical-body limit with the dissimilar case. Explain what further evidence is needed for oxide layers, adsorbed films, electrolytes and specific chemical interactions. The course PDF's extracted text loses some radicals; consult the displayed page before transcribing it.

